WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.420
- [Kelly] It's lovely
to have you all here.

00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:02.253
Do y'all get to, like,

00:00:02.253 --> 00:00:04.340
how often do you feel like you
get to actually hang out in

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.581
life? Are you both busy or
you get to really chill?

00:00:06.581 --> 00:00:07.680
- [Ron] Well in COVID of course,

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:08.960
we wrote this book during COVID,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.510
- [Kelly] A lot of chill there.

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:13.390
- but, but you know, we've been, I mean,

00:00:13.390 --> 00:00:15.470
we see each other whenever
we can, but there, you know,

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:17.160
there are stretches that go by and months

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.293
where we don't see each other.
- [Clint] But does it count

00:00:19.293 --> 00:00:21.210
when you have those zoom birthday parties

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.370
where you're hang the birthday sign above?

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:25.850
Hey bud, how you doing?

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:27.395
- [Ron] I don't think

00:00:27.395 --> 00:00:29.062
I don't think, she said,
that's what she's referring to.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:31.140
Part of I've been looking forward to

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:32.083
what we're doing right now,

00:00:32.083 --> 00:00:34.085
which is, you know, out together

00:00:34.085 --> 00:00:38.060
talking about this book for
like, since we started it,

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.870
because the book is very
cathartic for us on the one hand

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.290
and meant a lot to do,

00:00:42.290 --> 00:00:45.300
but also I just knew it was
going to be a great excuse for

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.750
us to hang and tell our
story a little bit to people

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:48.583
together.

00:00:48.583 --> 00:00:50.019
- [Kelly] I think that's so cool and I...

00:00:50.019 --> 00:00:51.184
(audience clapping)

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:52.630
- And I, I love, I feel like was it 2017?

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:53.880
Y'all lost your daddy

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:56.250
and then like 2000 maybe
you lost your mama.

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:58.320
So it's like, is it, was it kind of,

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.410
did you want to kind of start
this book and do all of this?

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:02.250
And it, was it kind of a
thing for your parents?

00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:03.083
Like a.

00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:04.660
- [Clint] Well that was
really the jumping off point

00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:07.835
for us kind of wanting to do
the book is when we lost dad.

00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:09.483
I mean, listen, to be honest,

00:01:09.483 --> 00:01:12.320
I don't do dying parents
very well. You know.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:13.350
- [Kelly] Oh, who does?

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.317
- Well, I don't know.

00:01:14.317 --> 00:01:17.048
- And if you do, there's
something wrong with you.

00:01:17.048 --> 00:01:18.400
(crowd laughing)

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:19.233
- But when, when,

00:01:19.233 --> 00:01:21.760
when dad passed and we
were actually kind of

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:22.970
organizing the memorial,

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.970
which meant going over to their house,

00:01:24.970 --> 00:01:26.333
our home that we grew up in

00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:28.515
and sifting through the photos,

00:01:28.515 --> 00:01:31.990
and items that we were going
to present at the memorial,

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.770
we just, they had such a
rich life and, and, you know,

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.220
it was originally, it was Ron saying,

00:01:38.220 --> 00:01:40.150
Hey, do you think you might
want to write a book with me?

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:41.620
And of course, you know, yeah.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:43.650
- I felt like with people, all
our lives we've been asked,

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.410
what was it like to grow
up in show business?

00:01:45.410 --> 00:01:46.970
What was it like to grow up on a TV set,

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.308
whether with Channel Ben or
Mayberry or whatever, and,

00:01:49.308 --> 00:01:53.120
and how did you get
through it? And I, I felt,

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:54.310
and I think I, this is what I said to you.

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.460
I think we can tell that story,

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.031
but we can really use it while
it's fresh in our mind to

00:01:59.031 --> 00:02:02.000
tell this other really surprising story,

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.440
because nobody knows about
this, this, you know,

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:05.640
the very specific,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.260
unique kind of funny
sometimes kind of odd ball.

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:09.093
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:09.093 --> 00:02:13.350
- But very effective choices
and decisions our parents made.

00:02:13.350 --> 00:02:14.980
So there's a way to honor them. And, and,

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:16.790
and hopefully answer that
question for those who are

00:02:16.790 --> 00:02:18.456
curious.
- [Kelly] Yeah. It must be.

00:02:18.456 --> 00:02:19.760
I love that it's called
The Boys. Is that it?

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.760
Did your mom call you that?

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:22.786
- That's what she called
all of us, the three of us.

00:02:22.786 --> 00:02:23.619
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:02:23.619 --> 00:02:24.760
- And so it's a tribute
to, a tribute to her.

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:25.880
- [Kelly] She's like
"where's some females?"

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.190
So Clint, going back to your childhood,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:31.180
Ron nicknamed you the hehe man.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:33.600
So I want you to tell this story about,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:34.939
cause you started naming him.

00:02:34.939 --> 00:02:37.950
- Yes. I gave him, I gave him the name

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.500
when he was really young,
like maybe 18 months old.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:42.320
- [Clint] Yeah, so I really
don't remember the incident.

00:02:42.320 --> 00:02:43.580
So let's let Ron tell this.

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:44.560
- [Ron] Alright. Okay.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.620
Well, first of all,

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:48.380
I was really thrilled to
have a younger brother,

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.100
very excited and, and you know,

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.320
and we were getting along fine.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.300
I was so excited when he
came home. We were, you know,

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.950
and then he bumps into the toddler.

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.160
I began to see that he's
got a real sense of humor.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:01.240
He's a real character. And one day though,

00:03:01.240 --> 00:03:03.710
he's now about 18 months old and I'm,

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:06.530
I'm down on the floor putting
together a puzzle or Lincoln

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:08.851
logs or something, which was
this thing we used to do.

00:03:08.851 --> 00:03:09.740
And,

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:13.660
and all of a sudden I feel this
warm liquid kind of landing

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:16.110
on my side of my face and down here.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:17.907
And I hear this hehehehe

00:03:19.037 --> 00:03:22.205
and I looked over and 18
month old Clint has dropped

00:03:22.205 --> 00:03:26.453
his diaper and kind of
like, just spraying me down.

00:03:27.338 --> 00:03:29.705
And I, I,

00:03:29.705 --> 00:03:31.363
- You know the foreshadowing was there.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:36.080
- I went to dad and I said,
you really got to spank him.

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.460
And he said, Nope, he's
too young to know better.

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.670
And that's what part of
it being a big brother is,

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:41.900
is you got to know and he's learning.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.733
- [Kelly] You gotta take the pee.

00:03:42.733 --> 00:03:45.100
- And you gotta take the pee.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.370
And so I was, you know,
just a little bit annoyed,

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:51.890
but I did appreciate the sense
of humor and the audacity.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:53.960
So from then on, he
became the Hehe-man to me.

00:03:53.960 --> 00:03:55.665
- [Kelly] I loved that it
wasn't the pee pee man.

00:03:55.665 --> 00:03:57.280
- (laughing) No, no, no.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.450
It's the laugh that I remember,

00:03:58.450 --> 00:04:00.380
I didn't want to remember the other.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:03.150
- That, that hehe kind of
sounded like pre-ice cream

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:06.981
like that, that man, that
was a little creepy then too.

00:04:06.981 --> 00:04:08.140
So wait, the book, the
book is very honest.

00:04:08.140 --> 00:04:09.760
I do want to talk about
the book. I love that.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.750
I feel like you shouldn't do
something like that unless you

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:13.050
are completely honest.

00:04:13.050 --> 00:04:15.690
And what I find amazing is
it's not always easy to talk

00:04:15.690 --> 00:04:16.874
about Clint's substance abuse

00:04:16.874 --> 00:04:19.120
and things and hard
trials that we go through.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:22.060
Was it a tough thing for you
to open up and kind of maybe

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.951
did a rehash like stuff, you know?

00:04:23.951 --> 00:04:27.043
- Well, to be honest, Kelly,
not at this point in my life.

00:04:27.043 --> 00:04:31.330
And also I realized that by me sharing,

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:34.240
by me telling my story
or part of my story,

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.560
it may help somebody, you
know, and listen, you know,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.510
I don't exactly know. I don't
blame show business at all.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:41.610
I really don't.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:46.610
But for some reason I had a
fascination with smoking pot

00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:50.290
when I was 13 or 14 years old. And I just,

00:04:50.290 --> 00:04:53.210
I got obsessed by the idea of
doing it and didn't have the

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:56.389
opportunity to do it right
away. But as it turns out,

00:04:56.389 --> 00:05:00.410
I was so obsessed that I took
the pencil shavings from my

00:05:00.410 --> 00:05:05.410
pencil sharpener in my
room and twisted up a fatty

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:06.253
- [Kelly] Well that's creative.

00:05:06.253 --> 00:05:10.230
- Yeah, and, and, and tried to smoke it.

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:13.140
Please folks, it's a little harsh.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.707
Don't try that at home.

00:05:14.707 --> 00:05:17.670
And, and, you know, and listen, you know,

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.170
through the travails of,

00:05:19.170 --> 00:05:22.940
of smoking weed or drinking
and the other stuff,

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:24.100
it's a slippery slope.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:26.930
And I was thrown down Crisco
and it was going pretty fast

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.823
and I'm very grateful, very
grateful to my mom and dad,

00:05:29.823 --> 00:05:32.770
you know, and in a backhanded way to Ron,

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:35.140
because Ron has a little
different take on it,

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:36.950
but he always supported me. And,

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.160
and one more thing I'll
say about it is, you know,

00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:41.130
I just didn't wake up
one morning and go, oh,

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.640
I've got a problem and stop. You know?

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:45.940
I mean, they talk about,
you know, hitting bedrock,

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:47.923
you know, and well, I, I scraped,

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.920
I scraped along bedrock for
awhile. So, you know, it's,

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:54.751
it's not pretty, but listen, I'm here.

00:05:54.751 --> 00:05:56.665
- [Kelly] But it's honest
and a lot of people,

00:05:56.665 --> 00:05:57.580
(crowd cheering)

00:05:57.580 --> 00:06:00.347
and I know a lot of people
that go through that same thing

00:06:00.347 --> 00:06:03.410
and I am with you. I
think that there it's,

00:06:03.410 --> 00:06:06.410
there's no point in hiding
it or, or under rug swept,

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:07.740
you know, keeping it under
swept I know a lot of people

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:08.573
in the South do that.

00:06:08.573 --> 00:06:10.290
And it's one of those things
where it's like, okay, no,

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:11.680
this is not your testimony.
This is not your,

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:13.490
your thing you can help other people with.

00:06:13.490 --> 00:06:15.010
And I think that's beautiful.

00:06:15.010 --> 00:06:17.500
That kind of raw honesty is so
helpful because it's not just

00:06:17.500 --> 00:06:20.120
about us. We're all a part of
something much bigger than us.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.580
And I think it's, it's really cool.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.123
And I think it's brave and bold.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:24.833
Yeah.

00:06:24.833 --> 00:06:25.666
- [Ron] I was very proud of him.

00:06:25.666 --> 00:06:26.499
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:26.499 --> 00:06:27.940
It's a hard, it's a hard thing.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:28.900
Well, you posted Ron,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.524
you posted this video of you
opening the first box of your

00:06:31.524 --> 00:06:34.010
new book with copies inside.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:35.980
So I do want to show you something though.

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:38.150
This is actually my
favorite part of this video.

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:40.590
So you take the book out.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:41.960
- [Ron] There's a picture
of the Andy Griffith show

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:42.793
- [Kelly] Yeah. Yeah.

00:06:42.793 --> 00:06:45.730
So cool. Then you turn and...

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:46.760
There we go.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:48.480
What's in it? What is

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:51.513
Now tell me Ron. What's
conveniently in the background?

00:06:51.513 --> 00:06:52.920
(Ron laughing)

00:06:52.920 --> 00:06:54.068
What is? Oh my God.

00:06:54.068 --> 00:06:56.847
You guys, these are my Oscars.

00:06:56.847 --> 00:06:57.680
Oh my God.

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:00.640
(audience clapping)

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.324
- [Ron] Okay. All right.

00:07:02.324 --> 00:07:04.940
Sin of pride. I do have an ego.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:05.773
- [Kelly] No it was amazing

00:07:05.773 --> 00:07:08.594
cause it was like, wait
let's just adjust here.

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:10.060
You're such a director. It was amazing.

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.280
I was like, hey, here's the thing.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:14.290
If I had an Oscar buddy,
I'd be having my foyer

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:15.560
Just people walked in. Oh, I just,

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.159
I was some, we have to take
it somewhere to clean it.

00:07:18.159 --> 00:07:18.992
There.

00:07:18.992 --> 00:07:19.900
It's an amazing award.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:21.110
And you have so many
amazing accomplishments.

00:07:21.110 --> 00:07:22.740
I just wanted to review that.

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:23.573
Well Clint,

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:26.560
Clint, it means you're family now.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.730
Clint and Ron's book is called The Boys,

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:30.670
a memoir of Hollywood and family.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.853
And it's out now. You
got to check it out guys.

